/*
===============================================================================
                         THREADS IN JAVA
===============================================================================

Thread:
A thread is a lightweight unit of execution.

A Java program can have multiple threads running concurrently.

Example:

        Main Thread
             |
      +------+------+
      |             |
   Thread-1      Thread-2
      |             |
    Task A        Task B


===============================================================================
                        THREAD LIFE CYCLE / STATES
===============================================================================

Java officially has 6 Thread.State values:

                    NEW
                     |
                     | start()
                     ↓
                  RUNNABLE
                  /      \
                 /        \
                ↓          ↓
          BLOCKED       WAITING
                \          /
                 \        /
                  ↓      ↓
                  RUNNABLE
                     |
                     ↓
              TIMED_WAITING
                     |
                     ↓
                  RUNNABLE
                     |
                     ↓
                TERMINATED


1. NEW
   Thread object is created but start() has not been called.

2. RUNNABLE
   Thread is ready to run or is currently running.
   Java combines "ready" and "running" under RUNNABLE.

3. BLOCKED
   Thread is waiting to acquire a monitor lock.

4. WAITING
   Thread waits indefinitely for another thread to perform an action.
   Example: wait(), join()

5. TIMED_WAITING
   Thread waits for a specified amount of time.
   Example: sleep(), timed join(), wait(timeout)

6. TERMINATED
   Thread has finished execution.


===============================================================================
                         RACE CONDITION
===============================================================================

Race condition occurs when:

    Multiple threads access shared data
    at the same time
    AND
    the final result depends on the timing/order
    in which the threads execute.


Example:

    Shared variable:
        count = 0

    Thread-1 → count++
    Thread-2 → count++


Expected:

    0 → 1 → 2

But internally:

    count++ is NOT one single operation.

It is approximately:

    1. READ count
    2. ADD 1
    3. WRITE count


Possible execution:

Thread-1                 Thread-2
   |                        |
 READ count (0)             |
   |                     READ count (0)
 ADD 1                      |
   |                     ADD 1
 WRITE 1                    |
   |                     WRITE 1


Final value = 1

Expected value = 2

This is a RACE CONDITION.


===============================================================================
                    RACE CONDITION - ACTUAL CODE
===============================================================================
*/

class Counter {

    int count = 0;

    // This method is NOT synchronized.
    // Multiple threads can execute it at the same time.
    void increment() {

        count++;
    }
}


public class ThreadDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {

        Counter counter = new Counter();

        // Thread 1
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {

            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                counter.increment();
            }

        });

        // Thread 2
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {

            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                counter.increment();
            }

        });


        /*
        =========================================================================
                                THREAD STATES
        =========================================================================

        At this point, both threads are in NEW state.

        start() changes the thread from:

            NEW → RUNNABLE
        */

        System.out.println("Before start:");
        System.out.println("T1: " + t1.getState());
        System.out.println("T2: " + t2.getState());


        // Starting the threads
        t1.start();
        t2.start();


        /*
        =========================================================================
                             JOIN()
        =========================================================================

        join() makes the main thread wait until the specified
        thread finishes.

        Without join():

            main()
              |
              +---- t1
              |
              +---- t2
              |
              +---- may print count before t1/t2 finish

        With join():

            main()
              |
              +---- waits for t1
              |
              +---- waits for t2
              |
              +---- prints final count
        */

        t1.join();
        t2.join();


        System.out.println("Final count: " + counter.count);


        /*
        =========================================================================
                            WHY IS THE RESULT WRONG?
        =========================================================================

        We performed:

            1000 increments in Thread-1
            1000 increments in Thread-2

        Expected:

            2000

        But because count++ is not atomic,
        the actual result may be less than 2000.

        Example:

            Expected = 2000
            Actual   = 1847

        The exact result can change every time.

        This is a RACE CONDITION.


        =========================================================================
                         FIXING THE RACE CONDITION
        =========================================================================

        We use the synchronized keyword.

        Change:

            void increment()

        to:

            synchronized void increment()


        Then only ONE thread at a time can execute increment().

        Thread-1                  Thread-2
           |                         |
        gets lock                   |
           |                         |
        increment()                 |
           |                         |
        releases lock               |
                                     ↓
                                  gets lock
                                     |
                                  increment()
                                     |
                                  releases lock


        This prevents multiple threads from modifying
        the shared variable simultaneously.
        */


        /*
        =========================================================================
                            SYNCHRONIZED EXAMPLE
        =========================================================================
        */

        CounterSafe safeCounter = new CounterSafe();

        Thread t3 = new Thread(() -> {

            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                safeCounter.increment();
            }

        });

        Thread t4 = new Thread(() -> {

            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
                safeCounter.increment();
            }

        });

        t3.start();
        t4.start();

        t3.join();
        t4.join();

        System.out.println("Safe final count: " + safeCounter.count);
    }
}


/*
===============================================================================
                     SYNCHRONIZED COUNTER
===============================================================================
*/

class CounterSafe {

    int count = 0;

    /*
    synchronized means:

    Only one thread can execute this method
    on the same CounterSafe object at a time.

    Therefore, the critical section is protected.
    */

    synchronized void increment() {

        count++;
    }
}


/*
===============================================================================
                         IMPORTANT TERMS
===============================================================================

THREAD
    ↓
Independent path of execution.


CONCURRENCY
    ↓
Multiple tasks make progress during overlapping periods.


SHARED RESOURCE
    ↓
Data/resource accessed by multiple threads.

Example:

    count


RACE CONDITION
    ↓
Multiple threads access shared data and the result
depends on execution timing.


CRITICAL SECTION
    ↓
Part of code where shared data is accessed/modified.


synchronized
    ↓
Allows only one thread at a time to enter the
synchronized section for the same lock.


join()
    ↓
Makes the current thread wait until another thread finishes.


sleep()
    ↓
Pauses the current thread for a specified time.

Example:

    Thread.sleep(1000);

    // 1000 milliseconds = 1 second


===============================================================================
                         THREAD STATE EXAMPLE
===============================================================================

Thread t = new Thread(() -> {

    System.out.println("Running");

});


                    t
                    |
                    ↓
                  NEW
                    |
                  start()
                    |
                    ↓
                RUNNABLE
                    |
             +------+------+
             |             |
          sleep()       waiting for
             |          synchronized
             ↓             |
       TIMED_WAITING    BLOCKED
             |             |
             +------+------+
                    |
                    ↓
                RUNNABLE
                    |
                    ↓
              task completed
                    |
                    ↓
               TERMINATED


===============================================================================
                    MOST IMPORTANT EXAM POINTS
===============================================================================

1. Thread is a unit of execution.

2. start() starts a new thread.

3. Calling run() directly does NOT create a new thread.
   
       t.run();      // normal method call
       t.start();    // starts a new thread

4. Java has 6 official Thread.State values:

       NEW
       RUNNABLE
       BLOCKED
       WAITING
       TIMED_WAITING
       TERMINATED

5. Race condition occurs because multiple threads access
   shared data without proper synchronization.

6. count++ is NOT atomic.

7. synchronized can protect a critical section.

8. join() makes one thread wait for another thread to finish.

9. sleep() puts the current thread into TIMED_WAITING.

10. A thread that has completed cannot be started again.


